\section{Counting Before Generating}

Breadth-first traversal may store exponentially many prefixes at a single depth. If only one word of a given length is needed, repeated suffix counts can replace that frontier. Let $C_k(q)$ be the number of accepted suffixes of length $k$ from state $q$ of a DFA. Then
\[
 C_0(q)=[q\text{ is accepting}],\qquad
 C_{k+1}(q)=\sum_{a\in\Sigma} C_k(\delta(q,a)),
\]
with an absent transition contributing zero. Even if two letters lead to the same state, count both: their suffix blocks have different first letters. The table lets an implementation skip entire lexicographic blocks to select a word by rank. The same sum over individual NFA edges would count paths instead, so determinize or count over reachable subsets first.

For scanner tests, bound the maximum length and record the alphabet; changing either changes what the suite covers. Positive examples alone do not test overacceptance. On a complete DFA, swap accepting and rejecting states to enumerate negative examples, or take a product of two recognizers and accept exactly when one accepts and the other rejects. The first product witness in shortlex order is a shortest reproducible disagreement. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-140} extends this idea to structured test generation, where a derivation and its expected tree matter as much as its terminal string.

