\subsection{Selecting a Rank with Saturated Suffix Counts}

Exact suffix counts grow quickly, even when the query asks for just
the fourth word of a given length. The selector only needs to know
whether a candidate block fits entirely before the current rank.
Counts can therefore be capped, provided the cap remains strictly
above the original rank and the same cap is used throughout the
table.

\textbf{Contract.} Given a complete DFA on a finite ordered alphabet,
a length \(\ell\ge0\), and a zero-based rank \(r_0\ge0\),
return the word of rank \(r_0\) among accepted words of length
\(\ell\), or report out of range. Build the suffix-count table
as above, but saturate each addition at \(B=r_0+1\) to obtain
\(C'_k(q)=\min(C_k(q),B)\). The implementation must compute
saturating sums without overflow before the cap is applied.

\begin{sudocode}[Select from capped lexicographic blocks]
\Let{(q,r,w)}{(q_0,r_0,\varepsilon)}
\If{$r\ge C'_\ell(q)$}
  \State \Return rank out of range
\EndIf
\For{$k=\ell,\ldots,1$}
  \For{each symbol $a$ in alphabet order}
    \Let{t}{\delta(q,a)}
    \Let{m}{C'_{k-1}(t)}
    \To{Skip only if the whole block precedes this rank.}
    \If{$r\ge m$}
      \Let{r}{r-m}
    \Else
      \To{Keep the local rank within the selected block.}
      \Let{(q,w)}{(t,wa)}
      \State break the symbol loop
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return $w$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} In the even-\(a\) DFA above, take length \(4\)
and rank \(2\), so \(B=3\). The \(a\) block has four words but
its capped count \(3\) still sends rank \(2\) into that block.
There the next \(a\) block contains exactly two words, so it is
skipped. The remaining rank is zero and the selected word is
\texttt{abab}.

At every step \(r\le r_0\), so a skipped block has \(m<B\)
and its count is exact. The maintained local rank is valid for
the selected suffix language, ending at an accepting state with
rank zero. Length zero thus selects only \(\varepsilon\) when
the start state accepts. Filling the table takes
\(O(\ell |Q||\Sigma|+|Q|)\) capped operations; selection takes
\(O(\ell|\Sigma|)\) with an appendable word buffer.
